Lichess Reputation v1.0.0 -- October 5, 2026

PURPOSE
Quantify observed future encounters and opportunities to return a kind draw;
estimate granting and the merit gradient when future encounters are unlikely;
and sharpen the first-versus-repeat comparison. These are descriptive tests
of particular strategic channels, not identification of a primitive motive.

RUN
Save the supplied ZIP in ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop and use the provided
checksum-verifying Terminal command. The launcher is RUN_REPUTATION.command.
It automatically selects an existing Python 3.10+ with NumPy, PyArrow and
DuckDB, runs the offline tests, logs the run and uses caffeinate on the Mac.
It installs no packages and makes no network/API requests.

Defaults: 4 workers, 4096 MB DuckDB memory limit, a fixed 1-in-5 unordered-pair
sample for history and regressions. The pair sample is outcome-independent;
both directions and all opportunities of a selected pair are retained.
Full-panel author-excluded counts and grant rates are computed separately.
This sampling rate is ten times the existing 1-in-50 first/repeat regression.
It does not imply a tenfold increase in independent information.

FIRST-RUN PLANNING ESTIMATE
About 4-10 hours on the Mac plus XT_Pro; UNBENCHMARKED for this new program.
The earlier cohort assembly took about two hours for its historical pair/
volume pass, but this program asks different queries and collects later
encounters. Drive throughput and other running analyses can change runtime.
Allow roughly 8-12 GiB total RAM for the largest serial numerical stages and
40 GiB free working space as a planning allowance, not a measured footprint.
The DuckDB memory setting is not a cap on NumPy or total machine memory.

Progress includes 45-second heartbeats during long serial stages, file/job
counts, reused checkpoints, stage ETA, and 25/50/75/100% stage milestones.
Milestones do not pause the run. ETA is for the current stage; heterogeneous
files and later model fits prevent a credible exact finish ETA at startup.
The program does not start or stop any other analysis or wait for its workers.

INPUTS AND REUSE
1. The pinned 24-month pool/nonadjacent main panel, 41,534,331 original rows:
   /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/derived/replication/
   analysis_panel_pool_nonadjacent_24m_v100_PRIVATE/panel/month=*/analysis_panel.parquet
2. The pinned original chronology manifest (852 files, original all-speed
   manifest count 7,763,847,245). This analysis uses its five live-rated speed
   categories; correspondence is excluded and the earliest observed month
   is reported. It checks the original file sizes, row counts and schemas,
   and binds every read to its current size/mtime fingerprint. It does not
   claim a fresh independent full-byte digest for billions of history rows.
3. Existing per-file first-pair caches from the repeated-exposure audit are
   reused where their query domain, configuration, source signature and
   output SHA agree. They supply minima only. New questions about later
   encounters and activity require a new projected pass of recent history.
4. INPUT_FILES.json of the pinned main-sample producer is used to resolve a
   byte-authenticated all-source Stage07 repayment panel when available.
   If this adapter cannot establish the full 47,587,020-row source, the run
   still produces explicitly labeled main-panel-only repayment results.
   Main-panel-only results are not an upper bound on all opportunities to
   reciprocate. INPUT_SCOPE.json records which branch ran.

No Stockfish searches, raw-PGN downloads, rating replays, IV score rebuilding,
or cohort reassembly occurs. Existing inputs/caches are read-only. New
products are in derived/replication/reputation_v100_PRIVATE. Checkpoints bind
inputs and code. Rerunning the same release and sample reuses completed work;
changing worker count or DuckDB memory preserves the scientific run identity.
Only incomplete outputs of this new run are rewritten on restart.

AUTHOR EXCLUSION
__PRIVATE_VALUE_1__ and __PRIVATE_VALUE_2__ are removed from focal and repayment opportunities using
trimmed, case-insensitive exact names, regardless of role. Their direct rows
are counted. Factual all-game chronology is preserved for meeting history
and actual playing volume. No synthetic replacement accounts are sampled.

OVERRIDES
  REPUTATION_PYTHON=/absolute/path/python /bin/bash RUN_REPUTATION.command
  REPUTATION_PROJECT_ROOT=/path/to/lichess_kindness /bin/bash RUN_REPUTATION.command
  /bin/bash RUN_REPUTATION.command --workers 4 --memory-mb 4096
  /bin/bash RUN_REPUTATION.command --pair-sample 5
The default Terminal command already sets the first resource options.
A denominator of 1 selects all pairs and can require substantially more RAM;
it is not the default and is not needed for this initial run.

WHAT TO RETURN
The printed LICHESS_REPUTATION_REPORT_<id>_<UTC>.zip and matching .sha256.
If it stops, also return the Desktop .log. A stopped run exports its reason
and preserves completed checkpoints. Reports contain aggregate tables and
design/QA information, not row-level histories or private account records.

READ THE OUTPUTS
full_panel_census.csv: full main-panel counts and rates after author exclusion.
reencounters_and_repayment.csv: fixed 30/90/180-day complete-window counts,
  meeting rates, reversed-role opportunities, observed reciprocation and
  rating-value accounting, by initial action, merit and prior relationship.
prediction_calibration.csv: predicted versus observed future meeting rates
  in distinct calibration/evaluation periods; includes absolute risk bins.
low_risk_grant_rates.csv: granting and merit contrasts in held-out low-risk
  observations, including first recorded meetings.
estimates.csv/json: adjusted first/repeat and low-risk merit regressions,
  with chooser CR1 and shared-account-or-date CR0 covariance variants.
timestamp_sensitivity.csv: encounter rates requiring a one-hour buffer after
  lastMoveAt as well as the unbuffered specification; tied starts are counted.
module_status.json: any unsupported prediction module remains explicit.

No empirical result is supplied in this code release. Validation uses
synthetic data and independent numerical checks, not your Mac's private rows.
See METHODS.txt for exact timing, prediction, inference and claim limits.
